(The Scrum Guide) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Scrum is a lightweight framework that helps people, teams and organizations) Tj T*
(generate value through adaptive solutions for complex problems.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The Scrum Team) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The fundamental unit of Scrum is a small team of people, a Scrum Team.) Tj T*
(The Scrum Team consists of one Scrum Master, one Product Owner, and Developers.) Tj T*
(Within a Scrum Team, there are no sub-teams or hierarchies.) Tj T*
(It is a cohesive unit of professionals focused on one objective at a time,) Tj T*
(the Product Goal.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Scrum Events) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The Sprint is the heartbeat of Scrum, where ideas are turned into value.) Tj T*
(They are fixed length events of one month or less to create consistency.) Tj T*
(A new Sprint starts immediately after the conclusion of the previous Sprint.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Sprint Planning initiates the Sprint by laying out the work to be performed.) Tj T*
(The resulting plan is created by the collaborative work of the entire Scrum Team.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The purpose of the Daily Scrum is to inspect progress toward the Sprint Goal) Tj T*
(and adapt the Sprint Backlog as necessary, adjusting the upcoming planned work.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The purpose of the Sprint Review is to inspect the outcome of the Sprint and) Tj T*
(determine future adaptations. The Scrum Team presents the results of their work) Tj T*
(to key stakeholders and progress toward the Product Goal is discussed.) Tj T*
ET
